{"name":"Longevity / Blue Zones diets","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Longevity_Hill_of_the_Summer_Palace.jpg/960px-Longevity_Hill_of_the_Summer_Palace.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Longevity_Hill_of_the_Summer_Palace.jpg","caption":"Longevity Hill of the Summer Palace","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":13,"casual":{"lead":["Blue Zones diets copy the largely plant-based eating of regions reported to have unusually many long-lived people, such as Sardinia, Okinawa and Nicoya. In appearance and longevity discussions they are used to stay lean and keep youthful-looking skin for longer.","The chain: plant-heavy food, physical activity, low stress and social ties are said to together support lower body fat and healthier aging, which shows in appearance. The idea originated in a 1999 Sardinia study, and Dan Buettner popularised it from 2005. Critics have questioned the accuracy of the age data, and reviews report no controlled trials of the diets; the diet's separate contribution has not been tested."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People build meals around beans, vegetables, whole grains, nuts and olive oil, eat meat sparingly, and often borrow the lifestyle habits such as daily walking and shared meals. It is followed as a long-term eating style."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["No specific harms are documented. Critics focus on the weak evidence and the commercial packaging of the concept."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Longevity_Hill_of_the_Summer_Palace.jpg/960px-Longevity_Hill_of_the_Summer_Palace.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/db/Longevity_Hill_of_the_Summer_Palace.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&ut